WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:02.733
[Narrator] Andre Ovredal's

00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.850
"Scary Stories to Tell in the Dark"

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:07.472
has brought the short
stories from Alvin Schwartz's

00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:09.850
nightmarish children's books to life.

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:10.860
And even if you were able

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.109
to keep your eyes open the entire time,

00:00:13.109 --> 00:00:15.492
there are a number of callbacks
to the original stories

00:00:15.492 --> 00:00:18.140
and other Easter eggs
scattered throughout the movie.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.040
Here's everything you might've missed.

00:00:21.308 --> 00:00:23.838
And warning: Spoilers ahead.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.890
While the books were
written in the early 1980s,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.740
the film is set in 1968.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.980
Producer Guillermo del Toro has said that

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.020
this date was chosen
because he considers it

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.490
an end of innocence for America

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.258
and that the Vietnam
War is a sort of ghost

00:00:41.258 --> 00:00:43.206
that looms over the town,

00:00:43.206 --> 00:00:46.532
with kids literally going
missing as they head off to war.

00:00:46.532 --> 00:00:49.080
There are a number of
references to this time period

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.680
and the war in the movie.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.330
The school is putting on the
1960s musical "Bye Bye Birdie,"

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.546
which was based on Elvis
Presley being drafted

00:00:56.546 --> 00:00:58.000
into the Army.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.430
In the movie, the character
Ramon is a draft dodger.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.530
On a school bulletin board,
you can see the lyrics

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.620
to the Pete Seeger song
"Turn! Turn! Turn!"

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.550
which was covered by The Byrds in 1965

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.690
and became popular as an anti-war anthem.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.950
The drive-in theater is showing
"Night of the Living Dead."

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.823
This classic black-and-white horror film

00:01:17.823 --> 00:01:21.701
was first released on October 1, 1968.

00:01:21.701 --> 00:01:25.423
Del Toro has described George
Romero as an iconoclast.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.270
The book featured in the
movie was actually inspired

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.670
by a book from another del
Toro movie, "Pan's Labyrinth."

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.390
In that film, the main character, Ofelia,

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:40.303
is given "The Book of Crossroads,"
which also writes itself.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.390
If you listen carefully, the
music actually changes slightly

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.390
each time a different story is
being featured on the screen.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.970
According to composer Anna Drubich,

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:53.337
"Harold" centers around guitar and banjo,

00:01:53.337 --> 00:01:56.307
"The Big Toe" is mostly brass,

00:01:56.307 --> 00:01:59.557
"The Red Spot" involves
string instruments,

00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:01.820
"The Pale Lady" has woodwinds,

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.470
and "The Jangly Man"
is heavy on percussion.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:05.927
Some of the characters in the film

00:02:05.927 --> 00:02:07.977
are directly from the original books,

00:02:07.977 --> 00:02:09.460
and others are new.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.870
The character who is chased
by Harold is named Tommy.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.010
In the books, he's named Thomas.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.740
But instead of transforming
into a scarecrow,

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.600
the character in the books
is skinned by the monster.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.310
Ruth is both the name of
the girl in "The Red Spot"

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:25.500
and the victim of the
spiders in the movie.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.790
The boy in "The Big Toe" has no name,

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:29.048
and neither do the characters

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:30.583
featured in "The Haunted House"

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:32.470
or "Me Tie Dough-ty Walker."

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.960
The monsters were all
created to closely resemble

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.360
the creepy drawings by
the book's illustrator,

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.550
Stephen Gammell.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.000
So the filmmakers stuck to
the black-and-white theme

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.454
as much as possible

00:02:42.454 --> 00:02:45.300
and desaturated the
colors on the creatures.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.210
And each monster is played by
a famous creature performer

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.970
that you have likely seen before.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.440
The actor who plays the
Jangly Man is Troy James,

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.750
a contortionist who has appeared
on "America's Got Talent."

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:59.567
Yes, he was actually
walking in that twisted,

00:02:59.567 --> 00:03:01.755
upside-down manner in the movie.

00:03:01.755 --> 00:03:05.790
The actor who plays the
toeless corpse is Javier Botet.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.400
He has Marfan syndrome, a genetic disorder

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.050
which makes his body longer and thinner.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.890
Botet has also played various
creatures in other films,

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.990
including "Slenderman,"

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.567
Melisandre's true form in season
eight of "Game of Thrones,"

00:03:19.567 --> 00:03:22.370
Mama in the del Toro horror film,

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.700
and the creepy sick hobo in "It."

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.373
Harold and the Pale Lady were
both played by Mark Steger.

00:03:28.373 --> 00:03:30.680
Steger was the man inside
the demogorgon costume

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.020
in "Stranger Things."

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.132
Sarah Bellows appears to be loosely based

00:03:34.132 --> 00:03:36.890
on a character in the
first book of the series,

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:39.350
from a story called "The Haunted House,"

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.240
in which a preacher
encounters a dead woman

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:42.620
who emerges from the basement

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:44.760
and wants revenge for her murder.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.855
But the drawing associated with this story

00:03:46.855 --> 00:03:48.560
was actually used as the face

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.730
of the toeless corpse in the movie.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.350
While most of the monsters in the movie

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.140
are straight from the
specific stories in the books,

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.743
the Jangly Man is actually a new character

00:03:57.743 --> 00:04:00.210
inspired by multiple creatures.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.277
In particular, he was taken from the story

00:04:02.277 --> 00:04:04.400
"Me Tie Dough-ty Walker,"

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.140
in which a severed head
falls down a chimney.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.120
In the original story, a dog responds

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.907
to the severed head's strange
chant with one of its own,

00:04:12.907 --> 00:04:16.360
"Lynchy kinchy colly molly dingo dingo,"

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.830
and it drops dead when the head appears.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:22.520
But in the film, it just
sort of growls and runs away.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.090
Auggie dresses up for
Halloween as a clown,

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.220
but not just any clown.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:29.230
The black-and-white suit
is the signature look

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:32.170
of the 17th-century clown Pierrot,

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:35.700
featured in performances
known as commedia dell'arte.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.100
When he's being chased through the house

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.541
by the toeless corpse,

00:04:38.541 --> 00:04:40.457
you can see a poster on his bedroom door

00:04:40.457 --> 00:04:43.147
featuring another standard
character from the genre,

00:04:43.147 --> 00:04:44.980
Arlecchino.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:48.308
The song that plays on Sarah
Bellows and Lou Lou's music box

00:04:48.308 --> 00:04:49.840
might sound familiar.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.330
It's called "The Hearse Song,"

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.350
which is famously featured
in the first book.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.160
It's based on a real song about death

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.570
sung by American and British
soldiers during World War I.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:02.320
At one point, the kids
visit Pennhurst Hospital,

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:05.340
the scene of Chuck's Pale Lady encounter.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.400
Pennhurst was a real asylum
in Spring City, Pennsylvania,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.560
that closed in 1987 and
has now been reopened

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.340
as a haunted attraction.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.340
When Stella is flipping
through Sarah's book,

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.700
we see several other stories
from the original books

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.580
that weren't featured in the movie.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.004
These include "The Wendigo," about a man

00:05:22.004 --> 00:05:24.645
who mysteriously disappears in the woods,

00:05:24.645 --> 00:05:26.111
"Strangers," about a man

00:05:26.111 --> 00:05:28.477
who meets a ghost on a train,

00:05:28.477 --> 00:05:30.438
"The Cat's Paw," involving a woman

00:05:30.438 --> 00:05:33.405
who turns into a cat and loses her foot,

00:05:33.405 --> 00:05:35.197
and "The Attic," in which a man

00:05:35.197 --> 00:05:37.904
is trying to find his
missing dog in his own attic,

00:05:37.904 --> 00:05:39.662
opens the door, and screams

00:05:39.662 --> 00:05:41.450
because he steps on a nail.

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:42.627
In the beginning of the movie,

00:05:42.627 --> 00:05:45.385
we see a short horror
story in Stella's bedroom

00:05:45.385 --> 00:05:47.701
that's not in the "Scary Stories" books

00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:51.060
called "The Whistling Room"
by William Hope Hodgson.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:52.890
And her bedroom is also full of shout-outs

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:56.380
to some obscure horror
films from the 1950s.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:57.987
There are posters from movies like

00:05:57.987 --> 00:06:01.987
"Beast from Haunted Cave,"
"Mesa of Lost Women,"

00:06:01.987 --> 00:06:05.179
"Frankenstein's Daughter,"
and "Indestructible Man."

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.120
Did we miss anything?

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.136
Let us know in the comments.

